$DIR/main.p:12:9: cannot return a + b, which points into memory allocated from ar, a local arena of f
    a + b points into memory allocated from ar, a local arena of f.
    alloc.Use(&ar) (at main.p:10) makes ar (a local arena of f) the default
    allocator, so what is allocated through the default allocator from there on
    (by new, a string operation, or a call that allocates and leaves out its
    allocator) lives in the arena, and is gone when the arena is. When f
    returns, the arena ar is gone, so a + b would point at memory that is no
    longer allocated.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Allocate what must outlive the arena from an allocator that does, passed
    explicitly:
        heap := alloc.Default() // before alloc.Use
        ...
        v := vec.New[T](heap)
    Or install an arena that outlives the result, such as one the caller passes
    in, or make the copy that must outlive the arena after the Restore is
    dropped.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
